Description: EGL on X11 lists its child window and keeps a stereo window one eye wide
 A stereo EGL window surface (EGL_EXT_multiview_window) on a child X window
 declared _KDE_NET_WM_STEREO_CONTENT but never listed itself in its owner's
 _KDE_NET_WM_STEREO_CONTENT_CHILDREN, so the compositor, which shows a child's
 own surface per eye only when it is listed, drew the packed buffer squeezed
 into the child. Mesa's GLX lists its children (patches 0017 to 0019); the
 calls are now one header, util/u_x11_stereo_child.h, that the EGL platform and
 the Vulkan paths share (a server grab around the read and write of the list,
 an owner found as GLX finds it, windows that are gone dropped when a child is
 added, the child kept while its window lives).
 .
 The EGL surface also writes _KDE_NET_WM_STEREO_CONTENT_VERSION 4 before the
 declaration when the compositor announces 4 or later, and then stops making
 the window twice as wide, as patch 0014 did for GLX: the window stays one eye
 wide, the buffer is twice its width (dri_drawable->stereo_window_one_eye), and
 the software path puts the packed image into a pixmap that it presents to the
 window. Before this the window of a stereo EGL surface was one eye twice, and
 an unmap and map of the window made the compositor double it again (640 wide
 became 1280, then 2560).
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-07
Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 via Claude Code
